'''Nagayama Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|長山県}}, ''Nagayama-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nakayagikoshu region|Nakayagikoshu]] in the mountainous interior of the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. With a population of 943,284 as of 2026, it is the only landlocked prefecture on Yagikoshu, entirely surrounded by the mountain ranges that form the island's spine. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Nagayama]], a hot-spring resort town and forestry centre nestled in the upper reaches of the [[Nagayama River]] valley.

Nagayama Prefecture is defined by its mountains and forests. Over 85% of the prefecture is forested, making it the most densely wooded prefecture in Okaiken and the centre of the nation's forestry industry. The prefecture is also famous for its {{nihongo|onsen|温泉}} hot springs—particularly the {{nihongo|Nagayama Onsen|長山温泉}} complex, one of the oldest and most celebrated in Yagikoshu—and for its {{nihongo|chestnuts|栗|kuri}}, which are among the largest and most prized in Adonia.

== History ==

=== The mountain kingdoms ===
Nagayama's mountainous terrain made it one of the last regions of Yagikoshu to be brought under central control. During the [[Sengoku period]], the prefecture's river valleys were controlled by a network of small {{nihongo|''yamashiro''|山城|mountain castles}} whose lords extracted tribute from the forestry and chestnut-growing communities. The {{nihongo|Nagayama Confederation|長山連合|Nagayama Rengō}}, a loose alliance of 14 mountain lords, maintained effective independence from the Honda clan until 1589, when [[Honda Shigetoki]]'s forces captured the key fortresses of [[Sekigahara]] and [[Tomioka]] in a brutal winter campaign.

The Confederation's resistance was remembered in local folklore as the {{nihongo|"Long Mountain War"|長山合戦|Nagayama Kassen}}, and the prefecture's identity as a place of stubborn independence and mountain self-reliance persists to this day.

=== Forestry and the chestnut tradition ===
Under the Honda Shogunate, Nagayama was designated as a {{nihongo|timber domain|材木領|Zaimoku-ryō}}, responsible for supplying construction timber to the shogunal capital. The prefecture's vast forests of Sugi cedar, cypress, and chestnut were systematically managed through a {{nihongo|rotation forestry|輪伐|rinbatsu}} system that is still in operation today, making Nagayama's forests some of the oldest continuously managed woodland in Adonia.

The chestnut—{{nihongo|''kuri''|栗}}—has been the prefecture's signature crop for over a thousand years. The {{nihongo|Nagayama Giant Chestnut|長山大栗|Nagayama Ō-guri}} is a landrace variety that produces nuts weighing 40–60 grams each, among the largest in the world. The chestnut was historically a staple food in the mountain communities, where rice cultivation was difficult, and chestnut flour, chestnut rice ({{nihongo|''kuri-gohan''|栗ご飯}}), and candied chestnuts ({{nihongo|''kuri-kinton''|栗金団}}) remain central to the prefecture's cuisine and identity.

== Geography ==

Nagayama Prefecture is entirely landlocked, occupying the mountainous interior of Yagikoshu. The prefecture is bounded by [[Mito Prefecture]] to the north, [[Kurosaki Prefecture]] and the [[Shendan Ocean]] coast to the east, [[Obama Prefecture]] to the south, and [[Saitama Prefecture]] and [[Kochi Prefecture]] to the west. The [[Suzuka Mountains]] form the prefecture's western boundary, while the [[Nagayama Range]] runs through its centre, creating a landscape of steep valleys and forested ridges.

The prefecture has no coastline, and its rivers—the [[Nagayama River]], the [[Shirakawa]], and the [[Hichiso]]—drain either east toward the Shendan Ocean or west toward the Sea of Mitoyagi. The mountainous terrain limits arable land to narrow river valleys, and the prefecture has the lowest proportion of agricultural land of any prefecture in Okaiken.

=== Climate ===
Nagayama Prefecture has a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] (''Cfa'') at lower elevations, transitioning to a [[w:Oceanic climate|oceanic climate]] (''Cfb'') in the highlands. The mountains create significant temperature variation: summer temperatures in the Nagayama valley reach 30°C, while winter temperatures in the highland communities regularly fall below −5°C, making Nagayama one of the coldest prefectures on Yagikoshu. Snowfall is heavy in the mountains, with some communities receiving over 3 metres annually.

The prefecture's abundant rainfall (over 3,200 mm annually in the highlands) feeds the rivers and hot springs, and the mist-shrouded mountain slopes create ideal conditions for forestry.

=== Hot springs ===
[[File:Nagayama Onsen Village.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Nagayama Onsen Village, a designated National Important Cultural Landscape]]
The {{nihongo|Nagayama Onsen|長山温泉}} complex is one of the oldest and most celebrated hot spring resorts in Yagikoshu, with records dating to the [[Heiji Period]] (794–1206). The complex consists of seven distinct spring sources, each with different mineral properties, ranging from sulphur-rich {{nihongo|''kakenoyu''|掛け湯}} baths to the iron-rich {{nihongo|''tetsuyu''|鉄湯}} said to be beneficial for joint ailments. The {{nihongo|Shirakawa Onsen|白川温泉}} in the eastern valley and the {{nihongo|Sekigahara Roten-buro|関ヶ原露天風呂}}—a remote open-air bath accessible only by a two-hour mountain hike—are also nationally famous.

The onsen industry is the prefecture's most important tourism draw, attracting approximately 3.2 million visitors annually and generating an estimated 円18 billion in revenue. The {{nihongo|Nagayama Onsen Village|長山温泉郷|Nagayama Onsen-gō}}—a cluster of 24 traditional ryokan inns along the Nagayama River—is a designated [[National Important Cultural Landscape]].

