== Economy ==

Nagayama Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: forestry, hot-spring tourism, and chestnut agriculture. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円28 billion in 2025, with services (primarily tourism) accounting for 52%, industry (forestry and wood processing) 28%, and agriculture 20%.

=== Forestry ===
[[File:Nagayama Forestry Cooperative.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Nagayama Forestry Cooperative, the largest in Okaiken]]
Forestry is the prefecture's dominant industry and its economic identity. The {{nihongo|Nagayama Forestry Cooperative|長山林業協同組合|Nagayama Ringyō Kyōdō Kumiai}} manages approximately 520,000 hectares of productive forest—roughly 85% of the prefecture's total land area—making it the largest forestry cooperative in Okaiken. The cooperative produces approximately 1.8 million cubic metres of timber annually, primarily Sugi cedar ({{nihongo|''sugi''|杉}}), cypress ({{nihongo|''hinoki''|檜}}), and Sanese chestnut ({{nihongo|''kuri''|栗}}).

The prefecture's timber is used in construction, furniture-making, and the production of {{nihongo|''sake''|酒}} barrels and {{nihongo|''shoyu''|醤油}} fermentation vats. The {{nihongo|Nagayama Cedar|長山杉|Nagayama-sugi}} is particularly valued for its straight grain and aromatic properties, and commands premium prices in the construction market. The {{nihongo|Tomioka Woodworking District|富岡木工地区|Tomioka Mokkō Chiku}} houses approximately 120 furniture and woodcraft workshops, producing high-end pieces sold across the [[East Fosian Sphere]].

The forestry sector employs approximately 12,000 workers directly and supports an additional 8,000 in processing and transport. The cooperative's annual revenue is approximately 円14 billion.

=== Chestnut agriculture ===
[[File:Nagayama Giant Chestnuts.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Nagayama Giant Chestnuts, among the largest in Adonia]]
The {{nihongo|Nagayama Giant Chestnut|長山大栗|Nagayama Ō-guri}} is the prefecture's most famous agricultural product and a GI-protected designation. The chestnut is cultivated on approximately 8,500 hectares of mountain terraces, producing approximately 45,000 tonnes annually—roughly 60% of Okaiken's domestic chestnut production. The nuts are sold fresh, dried, and processed into a wide range of products including {{nihongo|''kuri-kinton''|栗金団}} (candied chestnuts), {{nihongo|''kuri-yokan''|栗羊羹}} (chestnut bean jelly), {{nihongo|''kuri-mochi''|栗餅}} (chestnut rice cakes), and chestnut liqueur.

The {{nihongo|Nagayama Chestnut Festival|長山栗祭り|Nagayama Kuri Matsuri}}, held each October, draws approximately 180,000 visitors and is the prefecture's largest cultural event.

=== Onsen tourism ===
The onsen industry is described above. In addition to the traditional ryokan inns, the prefecture has increasingly developed {{nihongo|''onsen wellness''|温泉ウェルネス|Onsen Werunesu}} retreats targeting the domestic medical tourism market, particularly the growing demand for {{nihongo|''tetsuyu''|鉄湯}} iron-rich baths promoted for their supposed benefits for cardiovascular health. The {{nihongo|Nagayama Onsen Medical Research Centre|長山温泉医学研究センター|Nagayama Onsen Igaku Kenkyū Sentā}}, affiliated with the [[Kochi School of Medicine]], conducts research into the therapeutic properties of the prefecture's mineral springs.
Nagayama is renowned for its upland forestry and heritage cedar-vat fermentation cellars. Forestry cooperatives sustainably manage mountain sugi cedar and hinoki cypress groves, operating specialized sawmills that supply clear-grained architectural timbers to temple restoration carpenters. In the river valleys, traditional soy breweries operate centuries-old wooden vats where koji-inoculated soybeans slowly ferment through two complete summer-winter seasonal cycles, producing unpasteurized shōyu and rich red miso free of synthetic preservatives.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Okaiken Railways]] [[Nagayama Line]] connects [[Nagayama Station]] to [[Toyozawa Central Station]] in approximately 75 minutes on express services, via the [[Suzuka Tunnel]]. The [[Shirakawa Branch Line]] provides access to the onsen villages of the eastern valley.

=== Road ===
The [[Suzuka Expressway]] crosses the prefecture from east to west, providing the primary road connection to Toyozawa and the Shendan coast. The [[Nagayama Mountain Road]] (National Route 42) traverses the highland passes, though winter closures are common.

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Nagayama's cuisine is defined by its mountains. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''kuri-gohan''|栗ご飯}}—chestnut rice, in which the giant Nagayama chestnuts are simmered with rice and a light soy dashi, producing a dish of extraordinary sweetness and depth. Other mountain specialities include {{nihongo|''sansai''|山菜}} (wild mountain vegetables), {{nihongo|''yamame''|山女}} (landlocked cherry trout), and {{nihongo|''hoba-miso''|朴葉味噌}}—miso and vegetables grilled on a magnolia leaf over a charcoal brazier, a dish that originated in the forestry camps.

The {{nihongo|Nagayama Soba|長山蕎麦|Nagayama Soba}}—buckwheat noodles made with chestnut flour mixed into the dough—is a distinctive local variation that is served at onsen inns throughout the prefecture.

=== Festivals ===
* {{nihongo|'''Nagayama Chestnut Festival'''|長山栗祭り|Nagayama Kuri Matsuri}} — Annual chestnut harvest festival (October), drawing approximately 180,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Sekigahara Mountain Fire Festival'''|関ヶ原山火祭り|Sekigahara Yamahi Matsuri}} — Winter solstice bonfire festival (June), in which mountain communities light massive brushwood fires on the ridgelines
* {{nihongo|'''Onsen Thanksgiving'''|温泉感謝祭|Onsen Kanshasai}} — Spring festival (April) at the Nagayama Onsen Village, giving thanks for the hot springs

